WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.199
Good morning, Hank; it's Monday. Please to
be ignoring the decapitated parrot in the

00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:05.120
background. Today's video comes to you in
four parts.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.360
Part one: my encounter with Greg Mortenson.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.990
So Hank, Greg Mortenson, the author of 'Three
Cups of Tea', has been much in the news lately.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:15.400
There are accusations that he fabricated part
of his memoir and that his charity doesn't

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.320
run as efficiently as it should. And Hank,
it so happens that I can contribute to that

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.529
conversation because I have met Greg Mortenson,
and I thought I would tell you the story of

00:00:23.529 --> 00:00:24.529
how that went.

00:00:24.529 --> 00:00:27.160
Okay, so a couple years ago, when 'Paper Towns'
came out, I went to a library convention,

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.970
and Penguin, my publisher, threw this author
dinner for like, eight authors with two hundred

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.810
or something young adult librarians.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:37.680
Important note: almost all young adult librarians
are ladies.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:38.700
Okay, back to the story.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.961
Right, so at this author dinner, I was one
of the authors and so was Greg Mortenson,

00:00:41.961 --> 00:00:46.530
and we each had our own table that was full
of librarians, and we ate dinner and drank

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:50.860
wine and chatted and then we had to give,
like, a two minute presentation. And then

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.650
I was like, "Oh, I wrote this book, it's about
Walt Whitman and imagining other people complexly,

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:55.850
and I hope you like it." And I sat down.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.890
And then right after me, Greg Mortenson got
up and he was like, "That book sounds interesting;

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:03.640
my book is about how I almost died and decided
to devote my life to education in Afghanistan."

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.390
And I'm sittin' at my table kinda drunk thinking,
"Ugh, I wrote a book with a bunch of pee jokes

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:13.210
in it, and this guy's out saving the world."
But then, I remember that Nerdfighters helped

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:16.170
rebuild a school in cyclone-ravaged Bangladesh.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.190
And I wanted to tell him about it, but he
was all the way across this huge room; I could

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:23.120
barely even see the guy, so I got the bright
idea to go talk to him during the cocktail

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.730
party portion of the program that followed
the dinner.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:30.270
So as soon as we're finished eating, I race
up to Greg Mortenson and I say "Ah, it was

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:33.260
so great to hear your speech, I love your
book", which I hadn't read.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:38.440
And Hank, as you well know, in this equation,
X equals not being able to shut up, so I start

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.860
talking to Greg Mortenson about this online
community, Nerdfighters, and all the cool

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.570
stuff that we're doing; we're building pond
sand filters in Bangladesh, we're doing Kiva

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.810
Micro-Loans, we built this school, it's so
awesome!

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.090
And I buy him a drink - the drinks were free,
but still. And at some point I realize that

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.500
I've been talking to Greg Mortenson for twelve
minutes, and I have not allowed him to say

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.049
a single word.

00:01:57.049 --> 00:02:00.870
So I say, "I'm sorry to go on so much, I'm
just a huge fan of your work."

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:07.450
And then he says, "Thank you, but I'm not
Greg Mortenson." Hahahahah.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:15.260
Ohhhh, because ninety-five percent of young
adult librarians are women, I just walked

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:21.000
up to the white guy, figuring that that would
be Greg Mortenson. And it wasn't.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.730
Ugh. The humiliation is still washing over
me, Hank.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:26.510
So, Hank, here's what I have to add to the
Greg Mortenson discussion: it seems like his

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.800
heart is in the right place, although that
doesn't always matter, and, uh, he looks like

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:31.940
a lot of other white guys.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.440
Part two: are you Dutch?

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.590
Not you, Hank, I know you're not Dutch, but
the other people, they might be. If you are

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.560
Dutch, please read the video info below; there
is relevant information for you.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.120
Part three: Hanko de Mayo.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.390
Hank, you're birthday is coming up, it's Hanko
de Mayo, May fifth. So Hank, you're turning

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:48.530
thirty-one. Usually, we do something really
nice for you on your birthday, except when

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:52.610
we forget, but this year, your birthday is
very inconveniently located for me because

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:56.761
I am desperately trying to finish this book,
so I have decided to outsource your birthday

00:02:56.761 --> 00:02:57.761
to Nerdfighteria.

00:02:57.761 --> 00:03:02.290
Nerdfighters, you will find a link in the
doobly-doo to a birthday thread in Your Pants

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.400
where we can discuss ways to make Hank's birthday
awesome.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.470
Hank, don't read it or I will gouge out your
eyes. Probably not; that's probably excessive,

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:11.470
but don't read it!

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:14.470
And lastly, part four: I'm gonna do a Question
Tuesday on Friday - things that don't make

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.420
sense to new viewers. So please leave your
questions in comments.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.629
Hank, don't forget to be awesome; I will see
you on Wednesday.

